    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b9668f2d9988488c64e2bb7edb55ae94b1b84b947e249fbc96c506b1c5095d4d.png)\n\nA manufacturer produces a triangular prism-shaped box to hold chocolate candies. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?",
    "worked_solution": "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 24+2\\times 45+54 \\\\  & =48+90+54 \\\\  & =192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
